I recently read a note from the [Clayton Christensen Institute](https://www.christenseninstitute.org/) about where AI innovation is being directed. Its point was simple: most of the attention is going toward people who already have access. We are making diagnosis faster for patients who already have a doctor and lesson plans better for students who already have a teacher. We are improving systems for the people those systems already serve.

There is nothing wrong with that. Better care, better education, and better tools are all worth pursuing. But the note asked a more interesting question: what about the people who have no doctor, no teacher, or no practical way into the market at all?

That question led me to the Institute's framework for three kinds of innovation: sustaining, efficiency, and market-creating innovation. I find all three useful, and I do not think they have to compete with one another. In a healthy business they can reinforce one another. But market creation is the one that sounds like the most fun to work on, and probably the most rewarding.

Sustaining innovation makes an existing product better for existing customers. A faster computer, a safer car, or a more capable piece of software fits here. This is the kind of improvement most of us picture when we hear the word innovation. It matters because products cannot stand still while customers and competitors move. It keeps companies useful and markets healthy.

It can also help make market creation possible. The capabilities developed while serving today's customers can become the raw material for something much more accessible later. Performance improves, knowledge accumulates, components become dependable, and what once required an expert can eventually be packaged into something simpler. A sustaining improvement is aimed at the existing market, but the technology and experience it creates do not have to stay there.

Efficiency innovation is about doing more with less. It lowers the cost of producing or delivering something, improves margins, and frees cash. That can mean automation, a better process, or a less expensive way to operate.

The Christensen Institute is careful to point out that efficiency innovations often reduce jobs when they are used only to serve the same customers with fewer resources. That consequence is real. But the cash they free is also fuel. A company can return it to shareholders, hold it, or spend it on more efficiency. It can also reinvest that cash in sustaining improvements and in the harder work of creating a new market. Efficiency does not guarantee market creation. It creates the capacity for it, and the allocation decision determines what happens next.

Market-creating innovation begins somewhere else. It starts with nonconsumers: people who would benefit from a product or service but cannot use it because it is too expensive, too complicated, too distant, or requires expertise they do not have. The work is not to persuade them to buy a slightly better version of an existing product. It is to remove the barrier that kept them out.

The Institute's note used an example I keep thinking about. One-on-one tutoring has always been a luxury for much of the world. Rori, an AI math tutor from Rising Academies, works through WhatsApp on basic phones and low-bandwidth connections. It reaches students in countries including Ghana, Sierra Leone, and Kenya who had little or no access to tutoring. That is not a premium tutoring product getting another feature. It is tutoring becoming possible where the previous alternative was nothing.

The same distinction appears in [Rodrigo Fernandes's article, “Stop asking whether AI will destroy jobs”](https://www.christenseninstitute.org/blog/stop-asking-whether-ai-will-destroy-jobs/). Fernandes argues that the employment effect of a technology depends on what we ask it to do. Use AI to serve the same customers with less labor and it acts as an efficiency innovation. Use it to remove a barrier for people who could never participate, and it can create new consumption, new infrastructure, and new work. The technology can be identical. The direction is a choice.

What makes market creation so compelling to me is that success means more than taking share from an incumbent. It means someone can do something they could not do before. A student gets help that was never available. A small business gets a capability that only a large company could afford. A patient gets useful guidance when the existing system offered only an expensive appointment or no care at all.

It also sounds like fascinating work. Nonconsumers are hard to see in the data because they do not appear in sales reports. Their absence is the whole point. Finding them requires curiosity about the workarounds people use, the things they go without, and the progress they are trying to make. Serving them requires more than making an existing product cheaper. The whole model may have to change: the product, distribution, pricing, support, and assumptions about what is essential.

That is a much richer challenge than adding one more feature for an already well-served customer.

I do not take the framework to mean that sustaining and efficiency innovation are lesser work. The three can form a useful cycle. Sustaining innovation advances what is possible. Efficiency innovation makes those capabilities less costly and frees resources. Market-creating innovation uses capability and resources to reach people the old market could not serve. A new market then creates its own customers, needs, and opportunities for sustaining improvements.

The cycle is not automatic. A cheaper operation can simply become a larger margin. A better product can remain expensive and exclusive. Turning either into access requires somebody to choose nonconsumption as the problem worth solving.

That is the choice I would love to get behind: not only making an existing customer's life a little easier, but making something useful possible for someone who currently has nothing.

